.SHORT TERM...
Issued at 147 PM PST Tue Nov 3 2026
(This evening through tonight)

Satellite imagery this afternoon shows an area of low pressure
moving into our northern outer waters, just outside of 60nm and west
of Sonoma COunty. THe main band  of rain has pushed inland with
showers lingering behind it. More to the west, where the low is,
cumulus clouds are forming along the cold front. Looking at the SPC
mesoanalysis page, just next to that region it shows around 100-150
J/kg of CAPE and some shear. The other convective parameters are not
as favorable such as positive LI and the lapse rates becoming more
stable. Think that these conditions will generally hold for the
afternoon as cloud cover filled ahead of the front. An isolated
rumble of thunder or two is possible over the waters, but generally
the thunder chance is less than 10%. Instead, we`ll likely see
additional rainshowers activity as the front approaches. Precip
chances taper off this evening, with dry weather expected for most
areas by the late evening or early hours of Wednesday morning.
Behind the front, look for clear skies tonight and near seasonal low
temperatures.

&&

.LONG TERM...
Issued at 147 PM PST Tue Nov 3 2026
(Wednesday through next Monday)

Wednesday, high pressure builds into the region leading to a stretch
of warmer and drier weather. Wednesday will be the start of the
warming trend; however, we`ll still be leaning seasonable to
seasonably cool. Thursday into the weekend, high temperatures slowly
climb leading to temperatures becoming unseasonably warm by the
weekend where temperatures could be 10 to 15 degrees above their
normals.

In addition to that, tides continue to trend on the higher end.
Opted to issue a Coastal Flood Advisory for the San
Francisco Bayshorelines from 6AM Wednesday through 1PM MOnday.
Minor to moderate coastal flooding is expected during this time
frame.

